<section class="chapter" id="chapter-1">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 01</span>
<h2>Introduction to Processes</h2>
<p>
        A process is a fundamental concept in operating
        systems. It represents a program that is currently
        being executed by a computer.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-1-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 1.1</span>
<h2>What Is a Process?</h2>
<p>
        A process is a program in execution. It is an
        active entity that uses computer resources
        such as CPU time, memory, and input/output devices.
    </p>
<p>
        When a user opens an application, the operating
        system creates a process to execute the
        application's instructions.
    </p>
<h3>Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Opening a web browser</li>
<li>Running Microsoft Word</li>
<li>Playing a computer game</li>
<li>Executing a Java program</li>
<li>Running a media player</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        When Google Chrome is launched, the operating
        system creates processes that allow the browser
        to operate.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-1-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 1.2</span>
<h2>Difference Between a Program and a Process</h2>
<p>
        A program is a collection of instructions
        stored on a storage device.
    </p>
<p>
        A process is the active execution of
        those instructions.
    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr>
<th>Program</th>
<th>Process</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Passive</td>
<td>Active</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stored instructions</td>
<td>Instructions being executed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stored on disk</td>
<td>Uses CPU and memory resources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Does not execute by itself</td>
<td>Managed by the operating system</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
<div class="note">
        A Java source file is part of a program.
        Running the compiled application creates
        a process.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-1-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 1.3</span>
<h2>Why Are Processes Important?</h2>
<h3>1. Multitasking</h3>
<p>
        Processes allow computers to run multiple
        applications during the same period.
    </p>
<h3>2. Resource Management</h3>
<p>
        The operating system allocates CPU time,
        memory, and other resources to processes.
    </p>
<h3>3. Program Execution</h3>
<p>
        Processes provide the environment required
        for programs to execute.
    </p>
<h3>4. Process Isolation</h3>
<p>
        Operating systems help protect processes
        from interfering with one another.
    </p>
<h3>5. System Efficiency</h3>
<p>
        Process scheduling helps improve the
        utilization of computing resources.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-1-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 1.4</span>
<h2>Real-Life Examples of Processes</h2>
<h3>Web Browsing</h3>
<p>
        A browser executes processes to manage
        web content and application functions.
    </p>
<h3>Document Editing</h3>
<p>
        A word processor executes instructions
        for editing, formatting, and saving documents.
    </p>
<h3>Gaming</h3>
<p>
        A computer game uses processing resources
        to handle input, game logic, and rendering.
    </p>
<h3>Programming</h3>
<p>
        When a student runs a program, the
        operating system creates a process
        to execute the compiled instructions.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-1-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 1.5</span>
<h2>How the Operating System Manages Processes</h2>
<ol>
<li>A user starts an application.</li>
<li>The operating system creates a process.</li>
<li>Memory and other resources are assigned.</li>
<li>The process becomes eligible for CPU execution.</li>
<li>The CPU executes the process instructions.</li>
<li>The process may wait for input or other resources.</li>
<li>The process eventually finishes or is terminated.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 2
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-2">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 02</span>
<h2>Process Concepts and Characteristics</h2>
<p>
        Processes contain several components and
        characteristics that help the operating
        system manage their execution.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-2-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 2.1</span>
<h2>Main Components of a Process</h2>
<h3>Program Code</h3>
<p>
        Contains the instructions that the
        process executes.
    </p>
<h3>Program Counter</h3>
<p>
        Identifies the next instruction
        to be executed.
    </p>
<h3>CPU Registers</h3>
<p>
        Hold temporary data and information
        needed during processing.
    </p>
<h3>Stack</h3>
<p>
        Stores function call information,
        local variables, and return addresses.
    </p>
<h3>Heap</h3>
<p>
        Stores dynamically allocated data.
    </p>
<h3>Data Section</h3>
<p>
        Stores global and static variables.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-2-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 2.2</span>
<h2>Process Identification</h2>
<p>
        Each process is assigned a unique
        identifier called a Process ID or PID
        within the operating system.
    </p>
<p>
        The PID allows the operating system
        to distinguish and manage processes.
    </p>
