<td>3072</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
        The identifiers above are illustrative.
        Actual process IDs are assigned by
        the operating system.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-2-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 2.3</span>
<h2>Process Resource Requirements</h2>
<p>
        Processes require resources to execute.
    </p>
<ul>
<li>CPU processing time</li>
<li>Memory space</li>
<li>Storage access</li>
<li>Input/output devices</li>
<li>Files and network resources</li>
</ul>
<p>
        The operating system coordinates
        these resources to support
        multiple processes.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-2-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 2.4</span>
<h2>Process Creation</h2>
<p>
        Process creation occurs when the
        operating system starts a new process.
    </p>
<h3>Reasons for Process Creation</h3>
<ul>
<li>A user opens an application.</li>
<li>A running process creates another process.</li>
<li>A system service starts.</li>
<li>A scheduled task begins execution.</li>
</ul>
<p>
        The operating system creates the
        necessary process information and
        prepares resources for execution.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-2-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 2.5</span>
<h2>Process Termination</h2>
<p>
        Process termination occurs when
        a process stops executing.
    </p>
<h3>Common Causes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Normal completion</li>
<li>User-initiated termination</li>
<li>Application errors</li>
<li>Operating system termination</li>
</ul>
<p>
        After termination, the operating
        system can release resources
        associated with the process.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 3
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-3">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 03</span>
<h2>Process States and Life Cycle</h2>
<p>
        A process passes through different
        states during its lifetime.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-3-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 3.1</span>
<h2>What Are Process States?</h2>
<p>
        Process states describe the current
        condition of a process during
        its execution life cycle.
    </p>
<p>
        The operating system uses these
        states to determine whether
        a process is running, waiting,
        or ready to execute.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-3-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 3.2</span>
<h2>Five Main Process States</h2>
<h3>1. New</h3>
<p>
        The process is being created.
    </p>
<h3>2. Ready</h3>
<p>
        The process is prepared to execute
        and is waiting for CPU time.
    </p>
<h3>3. Running</h3>
<p>
        The CPU is currently executing
        the process instructions.
    </p>
<h3>4. Waiting or Blocked</h3>
<p>
        The process is waiting for an event
        or resource, such as input/output completion.
    </p>
<h3>5. Terminated</h3>
<p>
        The process has completed or
        stopped its execution.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-3-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 3.3</span>
<h2>Process State Transitions</h2>
<pre>
NEW
 |
 v
READY --------&gt; RUNNING
  ^                |
  |                |
  |          +-----+-----+
  |          |           |
  |          v           v
  |       WAITING     TERMINATED
  |          |
  +----------+
    </pre>
<p>
        A running process may return to
        the ready state when it is preempted.
        It may also enter the waiting state
        while waiting for an event.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-3-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 3.4</span>
<h2>Example of Process States</h2>
<ol>
<li>A user opens a text editor: New.</li>
<li>The application waits for CPU time: Ready.</li>
<li>The CPU executes instructions: Running.</li>
<li>The application waits for disk access: Waiting.</li>
<li>Disk access finishes: Ready.</li>
<li>The application resumes execution: Running.</li>
<li>The application closes: Terminated.</li>
</ol>
</section>
