<section class="chapter" id="chapter-4">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 04</span>
<h2>Process Control Block (PCB)</h2>
<p>
        The operating system maintains
        information about each process
        using a Process Control Block.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-4-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 4.1</span>
<h2>What Is a Process Control Block?</h2>
<p>
        A Process Control Block (PCB) is
        a data structure maintained by the
        operating system to store important
        information about a process.
    </p>
<p>
        The PCB allows the operating system
        to manage, schedule, and monitor
        process execution.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-4-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 4.2</span>
<h2>Information Stored in a PCB</h2>
<ul>
<li>Process ID</li>
<li>Process state</li>
<li>Program counter</li>
<li>CPU register values</li>
<li>CPU scheduling information</li>
<li>Memory management information</li>
<li>Accounting information</li>
<li>Input/output status information</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-4-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 4.3</span>
<h2>Importance of the Process Control Block</h2>
<p>
        The PCB allows the operating system
        to save and restore process information.
    </p>
<h3>Functions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Tracks process states.</li>
<li>Supports CPU scheduling.</li>
<li>Stores execution information.</li>
<li>Supports context switching.</li>
<li>Helps manage process resources.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-4-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 4.4</span>
<h2>Example of a Process Control Block</h2>
