</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-4-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 4.5</span>
<h2>Context Switching</h2>
<p>
        Context switching occurs when the
        CPU switches from executing one
        process or thread to another.
    </p>
<h3>How Context Switching Works</h3>
<ol>
<li>The operating system interrupts or pauses the current execution.</li>
<li>The current execution context is saved.</li>
<li>The scheduler selects another runnable task.</li>
<li>The selected task's context is restored.</li>
<li>Execution resumes.</li>
</ol>
<p>
        Context switching supports multitasking,
        but frequent switching creates overhead.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 5
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-5">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 05</span>
<h2>Introduction to Threads</h2>
<p>
        Threads allow a process to perform
        multiple activities concurrently.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-5-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 5.1</span>
<h2>What Is a Thread?</h2>
<p>
        A thread is the smallest unit of
        execution that can be independently
        scheduled within a process.
    </p>
<p>
        A process can contain one or
        multiple threads.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        A media player may use different
        threads to handle user input,
        audio playback, and video processing.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-5-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 5.2</span>
<h2>Why Are Threads Used?</h2>
<h3>1. Responsiveness</h3>
<p>
        Threads help applications remain
        responsive while other work continues.
    </p>
<h3>2. Resource Sharing</h3>
<p>
        Threads within the same process
        can share memory and other resources.
    </p>
<h3>3. Concurrent Execution</h3>
<p>
        Multiple threads can make progress
        during overlapping time periods.
    </p>
<h3>4. Parallelism</h3>
<p>
        On multicore processors, different
        threads may execute simultaneously.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-5-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 5.3</span>
<h2>Characteristics of Threads</h2>
<ul>
<li>Threads belong to a process.</li>
<li>Threads share the process address space.</li>
<li>Each thread has its own execution context.</li>
<li>Threads may execute concurrently.</li>
<li>Threads can communicate through shared memory.</li>
<li>Shared data may require synchronization.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-5-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 5.4</span>
<h2>Components of a Thread</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Thread ID:</strong> Identifies a thread.</li>
<li><strong>Program Counter:</strong> Tracks the next instruction.</li>
<li><strong>CPU Registers:</strong> Store execution values.</li>
<li><strong>Stack:</strong> Stores function calls and local data.</li>
<li><strong>Thread State:</strong> Indicates its execution condition.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-5-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 5.5</span>
<h2>Examples of Threads in Real Applications</h2>
<h3>Web Browser</h3>
<p>
        Threads may handle rendering,
        user interaction, and background work.
    </p>
<h3>Video Game</h3>
<p>
        Threads may handle game logic,
        audio processing, and resource loading.
    </p>
<h3>Media Player</h3>
<p>
        Threads can support audio playback,
        video decoding, and user controls.
    </p>
<h3>Programming Application</h3>
<p>
        An IDE may use background threads
        for code analysis, indexing,
        and other development tasks.
    </p>
</section>
