<section class="chapter" id="chapter-6">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 06</span>
<h2>Types of Threads</h2>
<p>
        Threads can be classified according to how
        they are managed by the operating system
        and the application.
    </p>
<p>
        The two primary types are user-level
        threads and kernel-level threads.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-6-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 6.1</span>
<h2>User-Level Threads</h2>
<p>
        User-level threads are managed by a thread
        library in user space rather than directly
        by the operating system kernel.
    </p>
<p>
        The thread library is responsible for
        creating, scheduling, and managing
        these threads.
    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thread creation can be fast.</li>
<li>Thread switching can require less overhead.</li>
<li>Thread management can be customized by applications.</li>
<li>Some operations do not require kernel intervention.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>In a many-to-one model, a blocking system call can block all user threads.</li>
<li>In a many-to-one model, multiple user threads cannot run simultaneously on different CPU cores.</li>
<li>The kernel may not recognize individual user-level threads.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        A user-space threading library may schedule
        several user threads over one kernel thread.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-6-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 6.2</span>
<h2>Kernel-Level Threads</h2>
<p>
        Kernel-level threads are managed directly
        by the operating system kernel.
    </p>
<p>
        The operating system is responsible for
        scheduling and managing these threads.
    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>The operating system can schedule threads independently.</li>
<li>Threads may execute simultaneously on multiple CPU cores.</li>
<li>Blocking one thread does not necessarily block the other threads of the process.</li>
<li>Kernel scheduling can support system-wide priorities.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Thread creation may involve additional overhead.</li>
<li>Switching between threads may require kernel operations.</li>
<li>Kernel-managed threads consume operating system resources.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-6-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 6.3</span>
<h2>Difference Between User-Level and Kernel-Level Threads</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>User-Level Threads</th>
<th>Kernel-Level Threads</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Management</td>
<td>User-space thread library</td>
<td>Operating system kernel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Creation</td>
<td>Often faster</td>
<td>May require kernel operations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Scheduling</td>
<td>Thread library</td>
<td>Operating system scheduler</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Blocking</td>
<td>Depends on thread mapping model</td>
<td>Individual threads can block independently</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Parallel Execution</td>
<td>Depends on mapping to kernel threads</td>
<td>Can run on multiple CPU cores</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-6-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 6.4</span>
<h2>Thread Mapping Models</h2>
<p>
        Thread mapping models describe the relationship
        between user-level threads and kernel-level threads.
    </p>
<h3>1. Many-to-One Model</h3>
<p>
        Multiple user-level threads are mapped
        to a single kernel thread.
    </p>
<pre>
User Threads:
T1    T2    T3
 \    |    /
  \   |   /
   \  |  /
 Kernel Thread
       K1
    </pre>
<p>
        This model is simple but does not allow
        those user threads to execute in parallel
        on multiple CPU cores.
    </p>
<h3>2. One-to-One Model</h3>
<p>
        Each user-level thread is mapped
        to a corresponding kernel thread.
    </p>
<pre>
User Threads:
T1    T2    T3
 |     |     |
 v     v     v
K1    K2    K3

Kernel Threads
    </pre>
<p>
        This model supports parallel execution
        but can require more kernel resources.
    </p>
<h3>3. Many-to-Many Model</h3>
<p>
        Multiple user-level threads are mapped
        to multiple kernel-level threads.
    </p>
<p>
        This model allows flexibility in scheduling
        and can support parallel execution.
    </p>
<h3>4. Two-Level Model</h3>
<p>
        The two-level model is a variation
        of the many-to-many model that can
        allow certain user threads to be
        bound to specific kernel threads.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 7
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-7">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 07</span>
<h2>Multithreading</h2>
<p>
        Multithreading allows a process to contain
        multiple threads that execute concurrently.
    </p>
<p>
        It helps applications perform several
        operations during overlapping periods.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-7-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 7.1</span>
<h2>What Is Multithreading?</h2>
<p>
        Multithreading is a programming and operating
        system technique in which a single process
        uses multiple threads of execution.
    </p>
<p>
        These threads share the resources of
        their parent process while maintaining
        separate execution contexts.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        A web browser may use multiple threads
        to process user interaction, load resources,
        and perform background activities.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-7-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 7.2</span>
<h2>Types of Multithreading</h2>
<h3>Concurrent Multithreading</h3>
<p>
        Multiple threads make progress during
        overlapping time periods.
    </p>
<p>
        On a single CPU core, execution may
        alternate rapidly between threads.
    </p>
<h3>Parallel Multithreading</h3>
<p>
        Multiple threads execute at the same time
        on different CPU cores or available
        hardware execution resources.
    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr>
<th>Concurrency</th>
<th>Parallelism</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tasks make progress over overlapping periods.</td>
<td>Tasks execute simultaneously.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Can occur on one CPU core.</td>
<td>Requires suitable parallel hardware.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Focuses on managing multiple tasks.</td>
<td>Focuses on simultaneous execution.</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-7-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 7.3</span>
<h2>Benefits of Multithreading</h2>
<h3>1. Improved Responsiveness</h3>
<p>
        Applications can respond to user
        actions while background work continues.
    </p>
<h3>2. Resource Sharing</h3>
<p>
        Threads within a process can share
        the same memory and resources.
    </p>
<h3>3. Better CPU Utilization</h3>
<p>
        Threads can use available CPU resources
        when other threads are waiting.
    </p>
<h3>4. Improved Performance</h3>
<p>
        Suitable workloads can benefit from
        concurrent or parallel execution.
    </p>
<h3>5. Efficient Application Design</h3>
<p>
        Complex applications can separate
        different responsibilities into threads.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-7-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 7.4</span>
<h2>Challenges of Multithreading</h2>
<h3>Race Conditions</h3>
<p>
        Race conditions occur when program
        behavior depends on the timing
        of concurrent operations.
    </p>
<h3>Deadlocks</h3>
<p>
        Deadlocks occur when threads or processes
        wait indefinitely for resources held
        by one another.
    </p>
<h3>Synchronization Problems</h3>
<p>
        Threads accessing shared data may
        require synchronization to prevent
        incorrect results.
    </p>
<h3>Debugging Difficulty</h3>
<p>
        Concurrent execution can make program
        errors difficult to reproduce.
    </p>
<h3>Context Switching Overhead</h3>
<p>
        Excessive thread switching can
        consume processing time.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-7-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 7.5</span>
<h2>Example of Multithreading</h2>
<p>
        The following Java example creates
        two threads that perform different tasks.
    </p>
<pre><code>public class MultithreadingExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread thread1 = new Thread(() -&gt; {
            for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
                System.out.println("Thread 1: " + i);
            }
        });

        Thread thread2 = new Thread(() -&gt; {
            for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {
                System.out.println("Thread 2: " + i);
            }
        });

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>
        The two threads can execute concurrently.
        The order of the displayed output may
        vary between program executions.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 8
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-8">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 08</span>
<h2>Processes vs. Threads</h2>
<p>
        Processes and threads are both essential
        components of modern operating systems,
        but they have different responsibilities.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-8-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 8.1</span>
<h2>Understanding the Difference</h2>
<p>
        A process is an independent execution
        environment with its own virtual
        address space and resources.
    </p>
<p>
        A thread is a unit of execution
        within a process.
    </p>
<p>
        Threads belonging to the same process
        normally share its memory and resources.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        A browser process may contain
        multiple threads responsible for
        different operations.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-8-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 8.2</span>
<h2>Comparison Between Processes and Threads</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Process</th>
<th>Thread</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Definition</td>
<td>Program in execution</td>
<td>Execution unit within a process</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory</td>
<td>Separate virtual address space</td>
<td>Shares process address space</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Creation</td>
<td>Usually more resource-intensive</td>
<td>Usually less resource-intensive</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Communication</td>
<td>Often requires interprocess communication</td>
<td>Can use shared process memory</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Isolation</td>
<td>Generally stronger isolation</td>
<td>Shares process resources</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Execution</td>
<td>Contains one or more threads</td>
<td>Executes instructions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-8-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 8.3</span>
<h2>Example of Processes and Threads Working Together</h2>
<p>
        Consider a computer running a browser,
        music player, and word processor.
    </p>
<h3>Processes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Browser process</li>
<li>Music player process</li>
<li>Word processor process</li>
</ul>
<h3>Threads Within the Browser</h3>
<ul>
<li>User interface thread</li>
<li>Rendering-related thread</li>
<li>Network-related thread</li>
<li>Background processing thread</li>
</ul>
<p>
        The operating system schedules the
        runnable threads while managing
        their process resources.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-8-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 8.4</span>
<h2>Why Both Are Important</h2>
<h3>Processes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Provide separate execution environments.</li>
<li>Support application isolation.</li>
<li>Help organize system resources.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Threads</h3>
<ul>
<li>Support concurrent execution.</li>
<li>Allow shared-memory communication.</li>
<li>Improve application responsiveness.</li>
</ul>
<p>
        Processes provide the execution
        environment, while threads perform
        the actual instructions within it.
    </p>
</section>
