     CHAPTER 9
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-9">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 09</span>
<h2>Introduction to Process Scheduling</h2>
<p>
        Process scheduling is one of the
        important responsibilities of
        an operating system.
    </p>
<p>
        It determines which runnable process
        or thread should use the CPU.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-9-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 9.1</span>
<h2>What Is Process Scheduling?</h2>
<p>
        Process scheduling is the method used
        by an operating system to select
        which ready process or thread
        should execute on the CPU.
    </p>
<p>
        Since many processes may compete
        for CPU time, scheduling helps
        organize their execution.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        When a student uses a browser,
        a music player, and a programming
        application, the operating system
        schedules their runnable tasks.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-9-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 9.2</span>
<h2>Why Is Process Scheduling Needed?</h2>
<h3>1. Efficient CPU Utilization</h3>
<p>
        Scheduling helps keep the CPU busy
        with useful work.
    </p>
<h3>2. Multitasking</h3>
<p>
        Multiple applications can make
        progress during overlapping periods.
    </p>
<h3>3. Fair Resource Distribution</h3>
<p>
        Scheduling policies can distribute
        CPU time among competing tasks.
    </p>
<h3>4. Better Response Time</h3>
<p>
        Interactive applications can receive
        CPU attention quickly.
    </p>
<h3>5. Improved System Performance</h3>
<p>
        Scheduling helps balance responsiveness,
        throughput, and CPU utilization.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-9-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 9.3</span>
<h2>The Role of the CPU Scheduler</h2>
<p>
        The CPU scheduler selects a runnable
        task from the ready queue.
    </p>
<h3>Main Responsibilities</h3>
<ul>
<li>Select the next task for CPU execution.</li>
<li>Apply the scheduling policy.</li>
<li>Support efficient processor utilization.</li>
<li>Coordinate with the dispatcher.</li>
<li>Help maintain system responsiveness.</li>
</ul>
<p>
        The dispatcher transfers CPU control
        to the task selected by the scheduler.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-9-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 9.4</span>
<h2>Ready Queue</h2>
<p>
        The ready queue contains processes
        or threads that are prepared to execute
        but are waiting for CPU time.
    </p>
<pre>
READY QUEUE

+--------+   +--------+   +--------+
|   P1   |--&gt;|   P2   |--&gt;|   P3   |
+--------+   +--------+   +--------+
                              |
                              v
                         CPU Scheduler
                              |
                              v
                             CPU
    </pre>
<p>
        The scheduler chooses a task
        according to the scheduling algorithm.
        The ready queue does not necessarily
        follow a first-in, first-out order.
    </p>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 10
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-10">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 10</span>
<h2>Types of Process Scheduling</h2>
<p>
        Operating systems use different types
        of schedulers to control process
        admission, execution, and suspension.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.1</span>
<h2>Long-Term Scheduler</h2>
<p>
        The long-term scheduler, also called
        the job scheduler, selects jobs
        from secondary storage and admits
        them into the system for execution.
    </p>
<h3>Functions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Controls admission of new jobs.</li>
<li>Influences the degree of multiprogramming.</li>
<li>Helps balance CPU-bound and I/O-bound workloads.</li>
</ul>
<p>
        Long-term scheduling is typically
        less frequent than short-term scheduling.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.2</span>
<h2>Short-Term Scheduler</h2>
<p>
        The short-term scheduler, also called
        the CPU scheduler, selects a ready
        task to execute on the CPU.
    </p>
<h3>Functions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Selects runnable tasks.</li>
<li>Determines CPU execution order.</li>
<li>Supports multitasking.</li>
<li>Runs frequently during system operation.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        The CPU scheduler selects between
        a ready browser task and a ready
        text editor task.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.3</span>
<h2>Medium-Term Scheduler</h2>
<p>
        The medium-term scheduler manages
        the temporary suspension and
        resumption of processes.
    </p>
<p>
        It may remove processes from main
        memory and later restore them.
    </p>
<h3>Functions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Supports process suspension.</li>
<li>Helps control memory pressure.</li>
<li>Can influence the degree of multiprogramming.</li>
<li>Supports swapping in systems that use it.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.4</span>
<h2>Comparison of Scheduling Types</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scheduler</th>
<th>Main Function</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Long-Term</td>
<td>Admits jobs into the system</td>
<td>Relatively infrequent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Short-Term</td>
<td>Selects tasks for CPU execution</td>
<td>Very frequent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Medium-Term</td>
<td>Suspends and resumes processes</td>
<td>As required</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.5</span>
<h2>Preemptive Scheduling</h2>
<p>
        Preemptive scheduling allows the
        operating system to interrupt
        a running task and assign the
        CPU to another ready task.
    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Can improve responsiveness.</li>
<li>Supports time-sharing systems.</li>
<li>Allows urgent tasks to receive CPU time.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>May increase context switching overhead.</li>
<li>Requires careful synchronization.</li>
<li>Can create starvation under some policies.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>Round Robin</li>
<li>Shortest Remaining Time First</li>
<li>Preemptive Priority Scheduling</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-6">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.6</span>
<h2>Non-Preemptive Scheduling</h2>
<p>
        In non-preemptive scheduling, a task
        normally retains the CPU until it
        finishes its CPU burst or blocks.
    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Simple to implement.</li>
<li>Generally requires fewer forced context switches.</li>
<li>Can reduce scheduling overhead.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Long CPU bursts may delay shorter tasks.</li>
<li>Interactive response time may be poor.</li>
<li>Some workloads experience long waiting times.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Examples</h3>
<ul>
<li>First-Come, First-Served</li>
<li>Non-Preemptive Shortest Job First</li>
<li>Non-Preemptive Priority Scheduling</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-10-7">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 10.7</span>
<h2>Difference Between Preemptive and Non-Preemptive Scheduling</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Preemptive</th>
<th>Non-Preemptive</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPU Interruption</td>
<td>A running task may be interrupted.</td>
<td>A running task generally continues until completion or blocking.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Responsiveness</td>
<td>Can provide faster interactive response.</td>
<td>May delay short or interactive tasks.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Context Switching</td>
<td>May occur more frequently.</td>
<td>Generally fewer forced switches.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Complexity</td>
<td>Often more complex.</td>
<td>Often simpler.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Examples</td>
<td>Round Robin, SRTF</td>
<td>FCFS, non-preemptive SJF</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="note">
<strong>Key Difference:</strong>
        Preemptive scheduling allows the
        operating system to take the CPU
        away from a running task.
        Non-preemptive scheduling does not
        forcibly interrupt a running CPU burst.
    </div>
</section>
<!-- ========================================
     CHAPTER 11
======================================== -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-11">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 11</span>
<h2>CPU Scheduling Algorithms</h2>
<p>
        CPU scheduling algorithms determine the order
        in which ready processes receive CPU time.
        Different algorithms use arrival times,
        burst times, priorities, or time slices
        to make scheduling decisions.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.1</span>
<h2>What Are CPU Scheduling Algorithms?</h2>
<p>
        CPU scheduling algorithms are methods used
        by the operating system to determine the
        order in which processes receive CPU time.
    </p>
<p>
        Different algorithms use different rules
        to select processes. Some prioritize
        the process that arrives first, while
        others consider execution time or
        process priority.
    </p>
<p>
        The main goal is to improve CPU utilization,
        reduce waiting time, and provide a smooth
        experience for users.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.2</span>
<h2>First-Come, First-Served (FCFS)</h2>
<p>
        First-Come, First-Served is a scheduling
        algorithm that executes processes according
        to their arrival order.
    </p>
<p>
        The process that arrives first receives
        the CPU first. Other processes wait until
        the current process finishes its CPU burst.
    </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Uses arrival order.</li>
<li>Simple to understand and implement.</li>
<li>Non-preemptive scheduling algorithm.</li>
<li>Processes are generally handled in a queue.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Easy to implement.</li>
<li>Fair based on arrival order.</li>
<li>Simple process management.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Long processes can make shorter processes wait.</li>
<li>May produce high average waiting time.</li>
<li>Not ideal for interactive systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        Three processes arrive in the following order:
    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr>
<th>Process</th>
<th>Arrival Time</th>
<th>Burst Time</th>
</tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>0 ms</td><td>5 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>1 ms</td><td>3 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3</td><td>2 ms</td><td>2 ms</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>Execution order:</strong> P1 → P2 → P3</p>
<h3>Gantt Chart</h3>
<pre>
+----------+--------+------+
|    P1    |   P2   |  P3  |
+----------+--------+------+
0          5        8     10
    </pre>
<h3>Step-by-Step Explanation</h3>
<ol>
<li>P1 arrives at 0 ms and executes for 5 ms.</li>
<li>P2 executes from 5 ms to 8 ms.</li>
<li>P3 executes from 8 ms to 10 ms.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Waiting Time</h3>
<p>P1 = 0 − 0 = 0 ms</p>
<p>P2 = 5 − 1 = 4 ms</p>
<p>P3 = 8 − 2 = 6 ms</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Average Waiting Time:</strong>
        (0 + 4 + 6) / 3 = 3.33 ms
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.3</span>
<h2>Shortest Job First (SJF)</h2>
<p>
        Shortest Job First selects the available
        process with the shortest CPU burst time.
    </p>
<p>
        In its non-preemptive form, the selected
        process continues until its CPU burst
        finishes or it blocks.
    </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Selects the shortest available CPU burst.</li>
<li>Can be implemented in preemptive or non-preemptive forms.</li>
<li>Helps reduce average waiting time.</li>
<li>Requires burst-time information or estimates.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Can provide low average waiting time.</li>
<li>Allows shorter processes to finish quickly.</li>
<li>Useful for predictable workloads.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Longer processes may wait for extended periods.</li>
<li>Exact CPU burst times are difficult to predict.</li>
<li>Starvation can occur.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        Assume all processes arrive at 0 ms.
    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr><th>Process</th><th>Burst Time</th></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>6 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>2 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3</td><td>4 ms</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>Execution order:</strong> P2 → P3 → P1</p>
<h3>Gantt Chart</h3>
<pre>
+------+----------+--------------+
|  P2  |    P3    |      P1      |
+------+----------+--------------+
0      2          6             12
    </pre>
<h3>Waiting Time</h3>
<p>P2 = 0 ms</p>
<p>P3 = 2 ms</p>
<p>P1 = 6 ms</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Average Waiting Time:</strong>
        (0 + 2 + 6) / 3 = 2.67 ms
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.4</span>
<h2>Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)</h2>
<p>
        Shortest Remaining Time First is the
        preemptive version of Shortest Job First.
    </p>
<p>
        It selects the process with the shortest
        remaining CPU execution time.
    </p>
<p>
        If a new process arrives with a shorter
        remaining time than the currently running
        process, the CPU may switch to the new process.
    </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Preemptive scheduling algorithm.</li>
<li>Considers remaining execution time.</li>
<li>Allows shorter processes to interrupt longer ones.</li>
<li>Can reduce waiting time for short processes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Prioritizes processes with less remaining work.</li>
<li>Can improve average waiting time.</li>
<li>Helps short tasks complete quickly.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Requires tracking remaining burst times.</li>
<li>May cause frequent context switching.</li>
<li>Long processes may experience starvation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr>
<th>Process</th>
<th>Arrival Time</th>
<th>Burst Time</th>
</tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>0 ms</td><td>8 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>2 ms</td><td>3 ms</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Step-by-Step Execution</h3>
<ol>
<li>P1 begins executing at 0 ms.</li>
<li>At 2 ms, P1 has 6 ms remaining.</li>
<li>P2 arrives with a burst time of 3 ms.</li>
<li>P2 has the shortest remaining time, so it preempts P1.</li>
<li>P2 completes at 5 ms.</li>
<li>P1 resumes and completes at 11 ms.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Gantt Chart</h3>
<pre>
+------+--------+--------------+
|  P1  |   P2   |      P1      |
+------+--------+--------------+
0      2        5             11
    </pre>
<h3>Waiting Time</h3>
<p>P1 = (11 − 0) − 8 = 3 ms</p>
<p>P2 = (5 − 2) − 3 = 0 ms</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Average Waiting Time:</strong>
        (3 + 0) / 2 = 1.5 ms
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.5</span>
<h2>Priority Scheduling</h2>
<p>
        Priority Scheduling selects processes
        according to their assigned priorities.
    </p>
<p>
        A higher-priority process is selected
        before a lower-priority process.
    </p>
<p>
        Priority values can be interpreted differently.
        In this example, a smaller number represents
        a higher priority.
    </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processes are assigned priority levels.</li>
<li>Higher-priority processes execute first.</li>
<li>Can be preemptive or non-preemptive.</li>
<li>May use aging to reduce starvation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Important tasks can receive CPU time earlier.</li>
<li>Supports priority-based task management.</li>
<li>Useful when tasks have different levels of importance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Low-priority processes may wait too long.</li>
<li>Starvation may occur.</li>
<li>Assigning appropriate priorities can be difficult.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr><th>Process</th><th>Priority</th></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>3</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3</td><td>2</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
        Assuming all three processes are ready
        and smaller numbers indicate higher priority:
    </p>
<p><strong>Execution order:</strong> P2 → P3 → P1</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Aging:</strong>
        Aging gradually increases the priority
        of a waiting process to help prevent starvation.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-6">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.6</span>
<h2>Round Robin (RR)</h2>
<p>
        Round Robin is a preemptive scheduling
        algorithm that gives each process a fixed
        amount of CPU time called a time quantum.
    </p>
<p>
        Each process receives a turn to execute.
        If it does not finish within its assigned
        time quantum, it is interrupted and placed
        at the back of the ready queue.
    </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Uses a fixed time quantum.</li>
<li>Gives processes turns to use the CPU.</li>
<li>Preemptive scheduling algorithm.</li>
<li>Commonly associated with time-sharing systems.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Provides regular CPU access.</li>
<li>Prevents one process from continuously occupying the CPU.</li>
<li>Suitable for interactive environments.</li>
<li>Allows processes to make progress.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>A small time quantum causes frequent context switching.</li>
<li>A very large time quantum makes it behave similarly to FCFS.</li>
<li>Performance depends on the chosen time quantum.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        Assume all processes arrive at 0 ms
        and the time quantum is 2 ms.
    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr><th>Process</th><th>Burst Time</th></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>5 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>3 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3</td><td>4 ms</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Execution Timeline</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr><th>Time</th><th>Process</th><th>Remaining Burst</th></tr>
<tr><td>0–2</td><td>P1</td><td>3 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>2–4</td><td>P2</td><td>1 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>4–6</td><td>P3</td><td>2 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>6–8</td><td>P1</td><td>1 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>8–9</td><td>P2</td><td>0 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>9–11</td><td>P3</td><td>0 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>11–12</td><td>P1</td><td>0 ms</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Gantt Chart</h3>
<pre>
+----+----+----+----+--+----+--+
| P1 | P2 | P3 | P1 |P2| P3 |P1|
+----+----+----+----+--+----+--+
0    2    4    6    8  9   11 12
    </pre>
<h3>Completion Times</h3>
<p>P1 = 12 ms</p>
<p>P2 = 9 ms</p>
<p>P3 = 11 ms</p>
<h3>Waiting Times</h3>
<p>P1 = 12 − 5 = 7 ms</p>
<p>P2 = 9 − 3 = 6 ms</p>
<p>P3 = 11 − 4 = 7 ms</p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Average Waiting Time:</strong>
        (7 + 6 + 7) / 3 = 6.67 ms
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-7">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.7</span>
<h2>Multilevel Queue Scheduling</h2>
<p>
        Multilevel Queue Scheduling divides
        the ready queue into several separate
        queues based on process categories.
    </p>
<p>
        Each queue may have its own scheduling
        algorithm and priority level.
    </p>
<h3>Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Uses multiple ready queues.</li>
<li>Groups processes by characteristics.</li>
<li>Each queue may use a different scheduling algorithm.</li>
<li>Queues may have different priorities.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Organizes different process categories.</li>
<li>Allows different scheduling policies.</li>
<li>Helps manage tasks according to requirements.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disadvantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Processes may be restricted to one queue.</li>
<li>Lower-priority queues may experience starvation.</li>
<li>Managing multiple queues can be complex.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Example</h3>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<tr>
<th>Queue</th>
<th>Process Type</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Queue 1</td>
<td>System Processes</td>
<td>System services</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Queue 2</td>
<td>Interactive Processes</td>
<td>Text editor</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Queue 3</td>
<td>Background Processes</td>
<td>File backup</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-11-8">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 11.8</span>
<h2>Comparison of CPU Scheduling Algorithms</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Algorithm</th>
<th>Main Rule</th>
<th>Type</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>FCFS</td>
<td>First process to arrive executes first</td>
