<section class="chapter" id="chapter-12">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 12</span>
<h2>Important Scheduling Terms and Performance Measures</h2>
<p>
        Scheduling performance is evaluated using
        measurements such as waiting time,
        turnaround time, response time,
        CPU utilization, and throughput.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.1</span>
<h2>Arrival Time</h2>
<p>
        Arrival time is the time when a process
        enters the ready queue and becomes
        available for CPU scheduling.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        If P1 enters the ready queue at 0 ms,
        its arrival time is 0 ms.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.2</span>
<h2>Burst Time</h2>
<p>
        Burst time is the amount of CPU time
        required by a process to complete
        a particular CPU burst.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        If a process needs 5 ms of CPU time,
        its burst time is 5 ms.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.3</span>
<h2>Completion Time</h2>
<p>
        Completion time is the time when
        a process finishes execution.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Example:</strong>
        A process finishes at 5 ms.
        Its completion time is 5 ms.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.4</span>
<h2>Turnaround Time</h2>
<p>
        Turnaround time is the total time
        between a process's arrival
        and its completion.
    </p>
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre>
Turnaround Time = Completion Time - Arrival Time

TAT = CT - AT
    </pre>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Arrival Time = 2 ms</p>
<p>Completion Time = 10 ms</p>
<pre>
TAT = 10 - 2
TAT = 8 ms
    </pre>
<div class="note">
        The turnaround time is <strong>8 ms</strong>.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.5</span>
<h2>Waiting Time</h2>
<p>
        Waiting time is the total amount
        of time a process spends waiting
        in the ready queue for CPU execution.
    </p>
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre>
Waiting Time = Turnaround Time - Burst Time

WT = TAT - BT
    </pre>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Turnaround Time = 10 ms</p>
<p>Burst Time = 4 ms</p>
<pre>
WT = 10 - 4
WT = 6 ms
    </pre>
<div class="note">
        The waiting time is <strong>6 ms</strong>.
    </div>
<p>
        This formula assumes the burst time
        represents the process's total CPU
        execution time without additional
        I/O waiting periods.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-6">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.6</span>
<h2>Response Time</h2>
<p>
        Response time is the time between
        a process's arrival and the first
        time it receives CPU execution.
    </p>
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre>
Response Time = First CPU Start - Arrival Time

RT = First Start - AT
    </pre>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Arrival Time = 0 ms</p>
<p>First CPU Start = 3 ms</p>
<pre>
RT = 3 - 0
RT = 3 ms
    </pre>
<div class="note">
        The response time is <strong>3 ms</strong>.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-7">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.7</span>
<h2>CPU Utilization</h2>
<p>
        CPU utilization measures the percentage
        of time the CPU is busy executing tasks.
    </p>
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre>
CPU Utilization = (Busy Time / Total Time) × 100
    </pre>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Busy Time = 80 seconds</p>
<p>Total Time = 100 seconds</p>
<pre>
CPU Utilization = (80 / 100) × 100
CPU Utilization = 80%
    </pre>
<div class="note">
        The CPU utilization is <strong>80%</strong>.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-8">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.8</span>
<h2>Throughput</h2>
<p>
        Throughput measures the number of
        processes completed within a
        specific period.
    </p>
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre>
Throughput = Completed Processes / Total Time
    </pre>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Completed Processes = 10</p>
<p>Total Time = 5 seconds</p>
<pre>
Throughput = 10 / 5
Throughput = 2 processes per second
    </pre>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-9">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.9</span>
<h2>Average Waiting Time</h2>
<p>
        Average waiting time is the average
        amount of time all processes spend
        waiting in the ready queue.
    </p>
<h3>Formula</h3>
<pre>
Average Waiting Time = Sum of Waiting Times
                       --------------------
                       Number of Processes
    </pre>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        Three processes have waiting times
        of 2 ms, 4 ms, and 6 ms.
    </p>
<pre>
AWT = (2 + 4 + 6) / 3
AWT = 12 / 3
AWT = 4 ms
    </pre>
<div class="note">
        The average waiting time is <strong>4 ms</strong>.
    </div>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-12-10">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 12.10</span>
<h2>Why Performance Measures Matter</h2>
<p>
        Performance measures help developers
        and system designers evaluate
        scheduling algorithms.
    </p>
<ul>
<li>Measure how efficiently CPU time is used.</li>
<li>Evaluate process waiting times.</li>
<li>Determine application responsiveness.</li>
<li>Measure completed work over time.</li>
<li>Compare scheduling algorithms.</li>
</ul>
</section>
