<section class="chapter" id="chapter-14">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 14</span>
<h2>Common Problems in Process and Thread Management</h2>
<p>
        Process and thread management can
        encounter problems involving scheduling,
        synchronization, and resource sharing.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-14-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 14.1</span>
<h2>Starvation</h2>
<p>
        Starvation occurs when a process
        waits for a very long time because
        other processes continually receive priority.
    </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        A low-priority background process
        remains in the ready queue because
        higher-priority processes repeatedly
        receive CPU time.
    </p>
<h3>Possible Solution: Aging</h3>
<p>
        Aging gradually increases the priority
        of a waiting process so it eventually
        receives CPU time.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-14-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 14.2</span>
<h2>Deadlock</h2>
<p>
        Deadlock occurs when two or more
        processes or threads become blocked
        because each is waiting for a
        resource held by another.
    </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<pre>
Process A holds Resource 1
Process A waits for Resource 2

Process B holds Resource 2
Process B waits for Resource 1
    </pre>
<p>
        Neither process can continue
        because each is waiting
        for the other.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-14-3">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 14.3</span>
<h2>Race Conditions</h2>
<p>
        A race condition occurs when
        multiple threads access shared
        information and the result depends
        on the timing of their operations.
    </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        Two threads attempt to update
        the same shared counter.
    </p>
<p>
        Without proper synchronization,
        one update may be lost.
    </p>
<h3>Possible Solutions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Mutex locks</li>
<li>Semaphores</li>
<li>Atomic operations</li>
<li>Proper synchronization</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-14-4">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 14.4</span>
<h2>Excessive Context Switching</h2>
<p>
        Context switching is necessary
        for multitasking, but excessive
        switching can reduce performance.
    </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>
        A Round Robin scheduler uses
        an extremely small time quantum.
        As a result, the CPU spends
        considerable time switching tasks.
    </p>
<h3>Possible Solution</h3>
<p>
        Select an appropriate time quantum
        and reduce unnecessary scheduling overhead.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-14-5">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 14.5</span>
<h2>Resource Contention</h2>
<p>
        Resource contention occurs when
        multiple processes or threads compete
        for limited system resources.
    </p>
<h3>Examples of Shared Resources</h3>
<ul>
<li>CPU time</li>
<li>Memory</li>
<li>Storage</li>
<li>Input/output devices</li>
<li>Network connections</li>
</ul>
<p>
        The operating system must coordinate
        resource access to maintain performance
        and stability.
    </p>
</section>
