<section class="chapter" id="chapter-16">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 16</span>
<h2>How Processes, Threads, and Scheduling Work Together</h2>
<p>
        Processes, threads, and process scheduling
        are connected concepts that allow modern
        operating systems to manage applications
        and computer resources efficiently.
    </p>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-16-1">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 16.1</span>
<h2>Understanding Their Relationship</h2>
<p>
        A process provides the execution environment
        for a running program.
    </p>
<p>
        Threads perform individual tasks within
        the process.
    </p>
<p>
        Process scheduling determines which eligible
        process or thread receives CPU time.
    </p>
<div class="note">
<strong>Key Concept:</strong>
        Processes organize application execution,
        threads perform tasks, and scheduling
        coordinates CPU access.
    </div>
<h3>Relationship Diagram</h3>
<pre>
        OPERATING SYSTEM
               |
               v
        PROCESS CREATION
               |
               v
          +---------+
          | PROCESS |
          +---------+
               |
       +-------+-------+
       |       |       |
       v       v       v
    Thread 1 Thread 2 Thread 3
       |       |       |
       +-------+-------+
               |
               v
         CPU SCHEDULER
               |
               v
              CPU
    </pre>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="section-16-2">
<span class="chapter-number">SECTION 16.2</span>
<h2>Step-by-Step Explanation</h2>
<p>
        Consider a user opening a web browser
        and playing a video.
    </p>
<h3>Step 1: Process Creation</h3>
<p>
        The user opens the browser.
        The operating system creates
        a process to execute the application.
    </p>
<h3>Step 2: Resource Allocation</h3>
<p>
        The operating system provides
        the process with memory and
        other required resources.
    </p>
<h3>Step 3: Thread Creation</h3>
<p>
        The browser uses threads to
        perform different activities,
        such as loading content,
        responding to user input,
        and processing background tasks.
    </p>
<h3>Step 4: Process and Thread Scheduling</h3>
<p>
        The operating system scheduler
        determines which eligible process
        or thread should receive CPU time.
    </p>
<h3>Step 5: Context Switching</h3>
<p>
        The CPU may switch between
        processes or threads so that
        multiple activities can make progress.
    </p>
<h3>Step 6: Task Completion</h3>
<p>
        When an activity finishes,
        its thread may terminate.
        When the user closes the browser
        and its remaining activities end,
        the associated processes can terminate.
    </p>
</section>
