<section class="chapter" id="chapter-17">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 17</span>
<h2>Summary and Key Learnings</h2>
<p>
        Processes, threads, and process scheduling
        are important concepts in operating systems.
        They explain how computers execute
        applications, manage tasks, and
        distribute CPU resources.
    </p>
<h3>Processes</h3>
<p>
        A process is a program in execution
        that requires memory, CPU time,
        and other resources.
    </p>
<p>
        It has its own address space
        and is managed by the operating
        system through information stored
        in its Process Control Block.
    </p>
<h3>Threads</h3>
<p>
        A thread is a smaller unit of execution
        within a process.
    </p>
<p>
        Threads share resources within their
        process but have their own stacks,
        registers, and program counters.
    </p>
<h3>Multithreading</h3>
<p>
        Multithreading allows applications
        to perform several activities
        concurrently or in parallel.
    </p>
<p>
        It improves responsiveness and
        can help applications use multiple
        CPU cores effectively.
    </p>
<h3>Process Scheduling</h3>
<p>
        Process scheduling determines which
        process or thread receives CPU time.
    </p>
<p>
        Scheduling algorithms such as FCFS,
        SJF, Priority Scheduling, and
        Round Robin use different rules
        to organize execution.
    </p>
<h3>Key Learnings</h3>
<ul>
<li>A process is a program in execution.</li>
<li>A thread is a unit of execution within a process.</li>
<li>Threads within one process share resources.</li>
<li>Multithreading supports concurrent activities.</li>
<li>The PCB stores important process information.</li>
<li>Context switching supports multitasking.</li>
<li>The CPU scheduler selects eligible tasks.</li>
<li>Scheduling algorithms use different selection rules.</li>
<li>Performance measures help evaluate scheduling.</li>
<li>Proper process management improves system responsiveness.</li>
</ul>
</section>
