<section class="chapter" id="chapter-18">
<span class="chapter-number">CHAPTER 18</span>
<h2>Key Terms and Definitions</h2>
<p>
        The following terms summarize important
        concepts discussed throughout this e-book.
    </p>
<div class="table-wrapper">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Term</th>
<th>Definition</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Process</td>
<td>A program that is currently executing.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Thread</td>
<td>The smallest unit of execution within a process.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Operating System</td>
<td>Software that manages computer hardware and software resources.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Process ID</td>
<td>A unique number assigned to a process.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>PCB</td>
<td>A data structure containing information about a process.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Process State</td>
<td>The current condition of a process.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ready Queue</td>
<td>A collection of processes waiting for CPU execution.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPU Scheduler</td>
<td>A component that selects the next process or thread for execution.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Context Switching</td>
<td>Saving one execution state and restoring another.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Multithreading</td>
<td>Performing multiple tasks using multiple threads.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPU Burst Time</td>
<td>The CPU execution time needed for a particular burst.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Arrival Time</td>
<td>The time a process enters the ready queue.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Completion Time</td>
<td>The time a process finishes execution.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Waiting Time</td>
<td>The time a process spends waiting for CPU execution.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Turnaround Time</td>
<td>The total time from process arrival to completion.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Response Time</td>
<td>The time from process arrival until its first CPU execution.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPU Utilization</td>
<td>The percentage of time the CPU is busy.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Throughput</td>
<td>The number of completed processes per unit of time.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Starvation</td>
<td>A situation where a process waits excessively for resources or CPU time.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Deadlock</td>
<td>A situation where processes wait indefinitely for resources held by one another.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Race Condition</td>
<td>A problem where results depend on the timing of concurrent operations.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Time Quantum</td>
<td>The fixed CPU time allocated in Round Robin scheduling.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Preemptive Scheduling</td>
<td>Scheduling that allows a running process to be interrupted.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Non-Preemptive Scheduling</td>
<td>Scheduling in which a process normally continues until its CPU burst ends or it waits.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Aging</td>
<td>A method of gradually increasing the priority of waiting processes.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
